<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
     AresLauncher row metrics, shared by the vertical home list and the app-list pane so both
     surfaces stay visually consistent. Niagara-style: small de-emphasised icon on the left, label
     as the dominant element, generous row height. See design/implementation-scope.md §8.

     These are a tuning starting point, not a derived spec. The sparse/text-forward look is a
     taste call. Adjust here and both surfaces follow.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Row height. Taller than a dense list row, well short of a grid cell.
         Reduced 64dp -> 52dp: on a real device the app list runs to hundreds of entries and 64dp
         rows made scrolling to the end take too long. 52dp still clears the 44dp minimum hit
         target with room to spare and keeps the airy, text-forward feel; going lower starts to
         read like a stock dense drawer, which is the look this deliberately avoids. -->
    <dimen name="ares_app_row_height">52dp</dimen>

    <!-- Icon is deliberately smaller than the stock all-apps grid icon (~48-56dp) so the label
         reads as the primary element. Trimmed with the row height so the icon keeps roughly the
         same proportion of the row rather than crowding it. -->
    <dimen name="ares_app_row_icon_size">32dp</dimen>

    <!-- Label is the dominant element, so slightly larger than the stock icon label. -->
    <dimen name="ares_app_row_text_size">17sp</dimen>

    <!-- Leading inset for the icon, and the gap between icon and label. -->
    <dimen name="ares_app_row_padding_horizontal">24dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_app_row_drawable_padding">16dp</dimen>

    <!-- Letter section headers (A / B / C) in the app list. Deliberately LIGHTER than an app row:
         smaller text, shorter height, de-emphasised colour. The letter is a signpost, not content,
         so it should recede next to the 17sp app labels it groups. Leading inset matches the row
         padding so letters and icons share one left edge. -->
    <!-- Trimmed alongside the row height: with ~26 letter groups in a real app list, header
         chrome is a meaningful share of total scroll length. -->
    <dimen name="ares_section_header_height">32dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_section_header_text_size">13sp</dimen>
    <!-- Extra breathing room above a header so each letter group reads as a distinct block.
         None below: the header should sit close to the apps it introduces. -->
    <dimen name="ares_section_header_padding_top">10dp</dimen>

    <!-- §11c "the top of the apps in the home page should line up with the top of the apps in the
         all list. Right now its a little off and it looks awkward".

         MEASURED on the emulator (Pixel 10 Pro Fold profile, 390dpi, density 2.4375) on 054a327,
         before any change. All values are absolute screen y of the *icon box*, not of the painted
         artwork; see the note below on why the box is the thing that can be aligned:

           folded    home first tile top 178, icon box +6   -> 184
                     app-list recycler top 152, pad +88, header +78, row icon +24 -> 342 (359 painted)
           unfolded  home first tile top 203, icon box +6   -> 209
                     app-list recycler top 203, pad +88, header +78, row icon +24 -> 393 (410 painted)

         So the two surfaces were 175px (folded) and 201px (unfolded) apart. Three separate
         contributions, only the first of which is a mistake:

           88px  the app-list recycler's stock top padding. It is
                 R.dimen.all_apps_search_bar_bottom_padding (36dp), reserved to clear a search bar
                 that this design does not show: §17 moved the affordance to a collapsed circle in
                 the bottom-right corner. Pure leftover; removed by these dimens.
           78px  the first letter section header. Structural, and it is what §11b fixes: with five
                 recents at the top of the list the first row is an app row, not a header.
           18px  the app row centres a 32dp icon in a 52dp row (+24px), while a home tile puts its
                 icon 6px below the cell top. That difference is what the two dimens below cancel.

         Why the icon BOX and not the painted pixels: the painted inset is a property of each app's
         adaptive icon, not of the surface. Measured on the same screenshot, Files paints full-bleed
         (146px in a 146px box) while Calendar paints at 60% (88px in the same box). There is no
         single painted offset to align, so the box (the frame every icon is drawn into) is the
         only well-defined target. A full-bleed icon then aligns exactly; a circular one is inset
         proportionally on both surfaces, so it lands ~5dp lower on home, where the icon is 60dp
         against the list's 32dp. That residual cannot be removed without making the two icon sizes
         equal, which would undo the Niagara look.

         Two values because the two app-list containers start at different heights. The unfolded
         AresPanelAllAppsContainerView is a page of the same Workspace as the home grid, so both
         recyclers begin at the same y (measured 203/203) and nothing has to be added. The folded
         sheet is a DragLayer child that begins at the status-bar inset (152) while the home page's
         CellLayout carries a further 26px of its own top padding (178), so the sheet has to make
         that up.

         Selected by which container it is, NOT by posture. An earlier revision branched on
         DeviceProfile.isTwoPanels and was measured wrong on the device: folded, the sheet's first
         row still came out at the panel's 0dp and sat 25px out. The flag itself is right (a smoke
         run in the same session read isTwoPanels:false folded, true unfolded), so what failed is
         freshness, not the flag: setupHeader() is where this padding is decided and it does not
         re-run on a posture change, so a posture-derived value is frozen at whatever the posture
         was when the header was last set up.

         Container identity has no such failure mode (a container's identity cannot go stale),
         and it is the more honest discriminator anyway, since the offset is a property of where the
         container is attached rather than of how the device is opened.

         Both gated on AresAllApps.isAresAppListPane, so the Taskbar's all-apps sheet and the
         secondary-display host keep stock padding. -->
    <dimen name="ares_app_list_top_padding_panel">0dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_app_list_top_padding_sheet">10dp</dimen>
    <!-- The 18px above, as dp. Applied to the home list rather than subtracted from the app list
         because the app list would need a NEGATIVE padding to absorb it when unfolded (both
         recyclers start at the same y there), and a negative padding enlarges a RecyclerView's clip
         box instead of moving its content. Costs 7dp of home grid height. -->
    <dimen name="ares_home_list_top_padding">7dp</dimen>

    <!-- §17 search affordance. Collapsed it is a circle of this size in the bottom-right corner;
         expanded it grows to the pane width less twice the resting margin. -->
    <dimen name="ares_search_collapsed_size">56dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_search_icon_padding">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_search_input_start_margin">24dp</dimen>

    <!-- Gap from the pane edges when resting. Clears the gesture-nav bar at the bottom. -->
    <dimen name="ares_search_margin_horizontal">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_search_margin_bottom">24dp</dimen>

    <!-- §6 widget resize affordance, shown on resizable widgets in edit mode only.
         28dp is below the 44dp hit-target minimum on purpose: the touch target is expanded to
         ares_widget_resize_touch_size by AresWidgetResize, so the glyph can stay small enough not
         to cover the widget it sits on while remaining comfortably tappable. -->
    <dimen name="ares_widget_resize_chevron_size">28dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_widget_resize_touch_size">48dp</dimen>
    <!-- Inset from the widget's bottom-end corner. -->
    <dimen name="ares_widget_resize_margin">4dp</dimen>

    <!-- Remove (x) affordance, shown on every home item in edit mode, top-start corner.
         Shares ares_widget_resize_touch_size and _margin so the two affordances are the same
         physical inset - they sit on opposite corners of the same tile, and a mismatch reads as a
         mistake.

         This is the size of the *drawn* badge, not of its touch target. The target stays 48dp; the
         badge is composed at this size and centred inside it (see ares_remove_badge.xml). Painting
         the backdrop across the full target instead made the circle nearly as wide as a 1x1 app
         icon, which read as a blob beside the icon rather than a badge on it. 28dp matches the
         resize chevron so the two corners carry equal visual weight. -->
    <dimen name="ares_remove_badge_size">28dp</dimen>
    <!-- The cross inside that circle. Inset enough to leave a visible ring of backdrop around it,
         so the glyph reads against a busy icon rather than blending into it. -->
    <dimen name="ares_remove_badge_glyph_size">18dp</dimen>

    <!-- How far the drawn badge is pulled toward its corner, away from the centre of its own touch
         target.

         The touch target has to stay 48dp for the hit-target minimum, but the badge drawn inside it
         is only 28dp, and centred it lands 34px from the tile corner (48dp box plus a 4dp margin,
         at 2.4375 density). A 158px icon centred in a 260px cell starts at 51px, so the two overlap
         and the badge reads as sitting ON the icon rather than in the corner of its tile - "the
         icons are kinda squished on top of the icons".

         Applied as padding on the two far sides, so the CENTER-scaled drawable shifts into its
         corner while the whole 48dp view stays touchable. 15dp puts the badge's outer edge about
         6px from the corner, which clears the icon on a 1x1 tile.

         Only possible now that a tile is its whole cell again: while tiles were inset to make a
         gutter, there was no corner left to move into. -->
    <dimen name="ares_badge_corner_pull">15dp</dimen>

    <!-- §14 / §21 edit-mode grid overlay. Both are drawn by AresEditGrid and are meant to read as
         one system: dots mark where things CAN go, the outline marks what an item currently DOES
         occupy. Kept small and faint on purpose - they sit under and over the icons respectively,
         and anything heavier competes with the content rather than describing it. -->
    <dimen name="ares_edit_grid_dot_radius">2dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="ares_edit_cell_outline_width">1dp</dimen>
    <!-- Matches the corner softness of the icon and widget shapes it traces, so the outline reads
         as the cell's edge rather than as a box drawn on top of one. -->
    <dimen name="ares_edit_cell_outline_radius">16dp</dimen>

    <!-- The dwell drop-target ring (AresFolderDrop). Heavier than the cell outline on purpose:
         the outline is a passive hint, this is the launcher answering "release now and it goes in
         here" after the user has deliberately held still for half a second. Shares the outline's
         corner radius so the two read as the same shape at different weights. -->
    <dimen name="ares_drop_ring_width">3dp</dimen>

    <!-- §23 Inset applied INSIDE a widget's holder, so adjacent widgets do not touch.

         Half comes off each side, so two neighbouring widgets end up a full inset apart while the
         grid's outer edge loses only half of one.

         Applied to widgets ONLY, and inside the holder rather than by shrinking the cell. Both
         parts were learned by getting them wrong:

         - Icons need no inset. They already draw centred inside their cell with their own padding
           (measured: a 158px icon in a 240px-wide cell leaves 41px a side), so the reported
           touching was always widget-to-widget - "widgets not apps are touching".
         - Shrinking the CELL to make the gap breaks two other things. The profile sizes icon
           content against the full cell (158px icon + 11px drawable padding + 39px label in a
           241px cell, ~13px of slack), so taking 20px out left the icon hard against the top of
           its tile and the edit-mode float carried it into the frost box edge. And an inset tile
           is no longer the cell it represents, which turned every gap into a dead zone for drop
           targeting - a full-width widget's centre landed in one, so dragging a widget chose no
           target and nothing reflowed.

         Not inherited from DeviceProfile.cellLayoutBorderSpacePx, which is the obvious source and
         measures 0.0px on both axes for this grid (the profile folds its spacing into cellWidthPx
         instead - 169px against our 260px masonry cell), so inheriting it would be silently
         indistinguishable from no inset at all. -->
    <dimen name="ares_home_widget_inset">8dp</dimen>
</resources>
